.Dd May 19, 2026
.Dt PS 1
.Os Substrate
.Sh NAME
.Nm ps
.Nd display process status
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Ar auxleb
.Op Fl p Ar pidlist
.Op Fl U Ar userlist
.Op Fl \-no\-headers
.Op Fl \-help
.Op Fl \-version
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility lists information about processes that exist on the system.
Process state is read through Substrate's
.Xr sys_proc_count 2 ,
.Xr sys_proc_list 2 ,
and
.Xr sys_proc_info 2
system calls; the command name when
.Fl e
is given is recovered through
.Xr sys_proc_cmdline 2 .
.Pp
By default,
.Nm
prints only processes that share the controlling terminal and effective
user with the invoker \(em a terminal-oriented short listing.
The selection and column layout are altered by the option letters
described in
.Sx OPTIONS .
.Pp
For compatibility with both major lineages of
.Xr ps 1 ,
the selection letters may be given without a leading dash and may be
clustered, so
.Ic ps aux ,
.Ic ps -aux ,
and
.Ic ps -a -u -x
are equivalent.
The same letters are also available as GNU-style long options
.Pq Fl \-all , Fl \-user , Fl \-long , Fl \-bitness , Fl \-environment .
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm a , Fl a , Fl \-all
Include processes that own a controlling terminal but are not session
leaders, regardless of owning user.
Combined with
.Cm x ,
selects every process on the system.
.It Cm u , Fl u , Fl \-user
Use the user-oriented preset.
Adds the
.Sy USER ,
.Sy PPID ,
.Sy VSZ ,
and
.Sy RSS
columns to the short listing.
.It Cm x , Fl x
Drop the controlling-terminal restriction from the default selection;
that is, include processes detached from a tty (typical daemons), but
still scope to the invoking user unless
.Cm a
is also given.
.It Cm l , Fl l , Fl \-long
Use the long preset, which expands the row to include
.Sy UID , Sy PGID , Sy SID ,
and
.Sy NI
along with everything in the user preset.
.It Cm e , Fl e , Fl \-environment
For the
.Sy CMD
column, render the full
.Va argv
recovered from the kernel command-line tail rather than the 16-byte
short name.
The argument vector is rendered NUL-separated as spaces.
.It Cm b , Fl b , Fl \-bitness
Insert a
.Sy BITS
column reporting the process's address-space width.
Values are
.Sy 16 ,
.Sy 32 ,
or
.Sy 64 ;
.Sy ?\&
appears when the kernel does not yet populate the field.
This is a Substrate extension.
.It Fl p Ar pidlist , Fl \-pid Ar pidlist
Restrict the listing to the comma-separated process IDs in
.Ar pidlist .
This overrides every preset-based selection rule.
.It Fl U Ar userlist , Fl \-User Ar userlist
Restrict the listing to processes owned by users in
.Ar userlist .
List elements may be numeric UIDs or login names looked up through
.Xr getpwnam 3 ;
unknown names are skipped silently.
This overrides every preset-based selection rule.
.It Fl \-no\-headers
Suppress the column header line.
Useful in scripts.
.It Fl \-help
Print a short usage summary and exit.
.It Fl \-version
Print the
.Nm
release identifier and exit.
.El
.Sh OUTPUT COLUMNS
The columns that may appear are:
.Bl -tag -width "USER" -compact
.It Sy USER
Login name of the effective UID (numeric UID when no
.Pa /etc/passwd
entry exists).
.It Sy UID
Numeric effective UID.
.It Sy PID
Process ID.
.It Sy PPID
Parent process ID.
.It Sy PGID
Process group ID.
.It Sy SID
Session ID.
.It Sy NI
Nice value, rendered as the kernel's 0..40 representation; the signed
\-20..+19 value is
.Sy NI \- 20 .
.It Sy TTY
Controlling terminal.
PTY slaves render as
.Pa pts/ Ns Ar N ;
virtual consoles render as
.Pa tty Ns Ar N ;
processes without a controlling terminal render as
.Sy ?\& .
.It Sy STAT
Process state plus modifier suffixes.
See
.Sx STATE CODES .
.It Sy BITS
Process bitness (16, 32, or 64), when
.Cm b
is given.
.It Sy TIME
Cumulative CPU time
.Pq Sy utime No + Sy stime ,
formatted
.Sy HH:MM:SS .
The conversion divides kernel-HZ ticks by
.Fn sysconf _SC_CLK_TCK ;
this is 128 on Substrate, not 100.
.It Sy VSZ
Virtual address-space size, in kilobytes.
.It Sy RSS
Resident set size, in kilobytes.
.It Sy CMD
Short command name from the kernel
.Va comm
field (or full
.Va argv
when
.Cm e
is given).
.El
.Sh STATE CODES
The
.Sy STAT
column starts with a single letter from the kernel state machine:
.Bl -tag -width "X" -compact
.It Sy R
Running or runnable.
.It Sy S
Interruptible sleep.
.It Sy I
Idle kernel thread.
.It Sy T
Stopped, typically by a job-control signal.
.It Sy Z
Zombie; awaiting reap by parent.
.It Sy X
Dying; in
.Xr proc_exit 2
teardown.
.It Sy ?\&
Unknown state.
.El
.Pp
The letter is followed by zero or more procps-style suffix characters:
.Bl -tag -width "<" -compact
.It Sy <
High priority
.Pq Va nice No < 0 .
.It Sy N
Low priority
.Pq Va nice No > 0 .
.It Sy +
The process is its own session leader and group leader \(em a
foreground process-group hint, since
.Xr ioctl 2
.Cm TIOCGPGRP
is not yet wired up to give a precise answer.
.El
.Sh SORT ORDER
Rows are sorted first by
.Sy TTY ,
then by
.Sy PID
ascending.
Processes with no controlling terminal (rendered
.Sy ?\& )
appear first.
.Sh DEFAULT SELECTION
With no options,
.Nm
shows only processes that satisfy both:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
Effective UID matches the invoker's effective UID.
.It
Controlling terminal matches the invoker's controlling terminal.
.El
.Pp
Either restriction is dropped by
.Cm x
(uid restriction) or
.Cm a
(tty restriction);
.Cm ax
selects everything.
Explicit filters
.Pq Fl p No or Fl U
override the preset selection entirely.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Show processes attached to the current terminal:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
ps
.Ed
.Pp
Every process on the system, BSD-style summary:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
ps ax
.Ed
.Pp
Every process, user-oriented with VSZ/RSS:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
ps aux
.Ed
.Pp
Full long listing with environment-expanded
.Sy CMD
and the Substrate
.Sy BITS
column inserted:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
ps auxleb
.Ed
.Pp
Just init and the user-space login shell, scripted (no headers):
.Bd -literal -offset indent
ps --no-headers -p 1,$$
.Ed
.Pp
Everyone but root:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
ps ax | awk '$1 != "root"'
.Ed
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
.Bl -tag -width "ps:"
.It Sy ps: failed to query current process
.Xr sys_proc_info 2
returned an error for the invoking process.
.It Sy ps: sys_proc_count failed
The kernel rejected
.Xr sys_proc_count 2 ;
typically only happens during very early boot before the process
registry is initialized.
.It Sy ps: sys_proc_list failed
.Xr sys_proc_list 2
returned an error;
.Nm
could not enumerate the active set.
.It Sy ps: out of memory
.Xr malloc 3
or
.Xr calloc 3
failed to allocate the per-process row buffer.
.It Sy ps: bad -p PID list
The argument to
.Fl p
contained a non-numeric token or a negative value.
.It Sy ps: bad -U user list
The argument to
.Fl U
overflowed the internal filter array.
.It Sy ps: unexpected non-option argument
A trailing positional argument was given.
Selection by PID requires
.Fl p ;
selection by user requires
.Fl U .
.It Sy ps: unknown option
A short option not in
.Sq auxlebpU
or an unrecognized long option was given.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits 0 on success or 1 on any failure to enumerate or render the
process set.
A non-empty listing of the requested set is not required for success;
.Nm
with no matching processes exits 0 and prints only the header.
.Sh NOTES
.Bl -bullet
.It
The
.Sy STAT
suffix
.Sy l
.Pq multi-threaded
is not yet emitted; per-process thread enumeration is pending in the
kernel ABI.
.It
.Xr ioctl 2
.Cm TIOCGPGRP
is not yet implemented, so the
.Sy +
suffix is derived from the
.Sy PID No = Sy PGID No = Sy SID
heuristic rather than from a real foreground-pgrp query.
.It
.Sy %CPU
and
.Sy %MEM
columns are not yet supplied; the kernel does not yet ship a recent
CPU-usage window.
.It
The
.Sy CMD
column when
.Cm e
is absent is capped at the kernel's 16-byte
.Va comm
field.
With
.Cm e
the full
.Va argv
blob is recovered through
.Xr sys_proc_cmdline 2 ,
which is currently capped at 512 bytes per process.
.It
.Nm
does not yet support the BSD
.Fl O Ar fmt
or System V
.Fl o Ar fmt
explicit column selectors.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr top 1 ,
.Xr kill 1 ,
.Xr getpwnam 3 ,
.Xr sysconf 3 ,
.Xr sys_proc_count 2 ,
.Xr sys_proc_list 2 ,
.Xr sys_proc_info 2 ,
.Xr sys_proc_cmdline 2 ,
.Xr proc 5
.Sh STANDARDS
.Nm
implements the option letters required by
.St -p1003.1-2024
and a substantial superset of the procps and BSD historical
extensions.
The
.Sy BITS
column and the
.Cm b
/ Fl \-bitness
selector are Substrate extensions.
.Sh HISTORY
A
.Nm
utility appeared in Version 3 AT&T UNIX.
The dashless BSD-cluster syntax originated with 1BSD; the
.Sy STAT
suffix conventions
.Pq Sy + , Sy < , Sy N
are from procps.
The Substrate implementation is a clean-room rewrite atop the
native
.Va sys_procinfo_t
ABI, sharing no code with either lineage.
.Sh BUGS
Cumulative CPU time wraps at 2\^32 ticks (about 388 days at 128 Hz)
because the kernel reports
.Va user_time
and
.Va sys_time
as
.Vt uint32_t
rather than
.Vt uint64_t .
Long-lived daemons will reset to 00:00:00 around that boundary.
